WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:05.076
IIIF Kasane: comparing the Shelley manuscript — Opening it from Use cases

2
00:00:05.079 --> 00:00:13.679
IIIF Kasane is a viewer that layers IIIF images, 3D models and annotations in a single view.

3
00:00:13.683 --> 00:00:18.483
The Use cases page lists examples with real collections.

4
00:00:18.488 --> 00:00:22.288
In this video, we use the Shelley manuscript.

5
00:00:22.293 --> 00:00:28.843
There are two photographs of the same sheet: one in ordinary light, and one in transmitted light.

6
00:00:28.845 --> 00:00:34.470
The transmitted-light image is taken with light shining through the paper from behind.

7
00:00:34.473 --> 00:00:39.423
Click "Open in Kasane" to open a page for comparing the two.

8
00:00:41.401 --> 00:00:46.026
IIIF Kasane: comparing the Shelley manuscript — Side by side

9
00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:51.380
In the bar at the top, you choose how to show the images, and which two to compare.

10
00:00:51.384 --> 00:01:01.234
Right now it is "Side by side". A is the original image, and B the transmitted-light one; the manifest names them in Japanese.

11
00:01:01.237 --> 00:01:06.762
The original image has been flipped upside down, to make comparison easier.

12
00:01:06.768 --> 00:01:09.368
Zoom in with the mouse wheel.

13
00:01:11.308 --> 00:01:14.408
Both sides zoom and move together.

14
00:01:17.038 --> 00:01:24.238
In the transmitted-light image, large pale letters appear. They are a watermark in the paper.

15
00:01:24.242 --> 00:01:29.117
In the same place in the original image, the letters cannot be seen.

16
00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:36.345
IIIF Kasane: comparing the Shelley manuscript — Swipe, spyglass and overlay

17
00:01:36.349 --> 00:01:40.774
In "Swipe", A is shown left of the line, and B to the right.

18
00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:47.155
Drag the line to the left, and the transmitted-light image spreads out, revealing the watermark.

19
00:01:47.162 --> 00:01:50.687
Drag it back, and the watermark disappears.

20
00:01:50.691 --> 00:01:55.141
In "Spyglass", B is shown only inside a round window.

21
00:01:55.143 --> 00:01:58.468
Move it to trace the watermark letters.

22
00:01:58.836 --> 00:02:02.786
The window can also magnify two or three times.

23
00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:07.520
In "Overlay", B is laid over A, half transparent.

24
00:02:07.524 --> 00:02:12.299
The "Opacity of B" slider fades from one image to the other.

25
00:02:14.068 --> 00:02:19.918
The mode is kept in the URL, so you can share a comparison just by sending the link.

26
00:02:21.901 --> 00:02:26.626
IIIF Kasane: comparing the Shelley manuscript — Where the data comes from

27
00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:30.880
The panel on the right shows the details from the manifest.

28
00:02:30.887 --> 00:02:36.062
The title is "Fragment of an Address to the Jews", by Percy Bysshe Shelley.

29
00:02:36.066 --> 00:02:41.491
The original is held by the University of Tokyo General Library.

30
00:02:41.493 --> 00:02:49.793
The manifest and the images are published by the source project. Both images sit on one canvas, as a choice.

31
00:02:49.794 --> 00:02:54.944
Kasane reads that choice, and turns it into the A and B options.

32
00:02:54.949 --> 00:03:00.724
For details of the manuscript and the terms of use, please see the source project's site.

33
00:03:00.726 --> 00:03:06.626
Any other manifest whose images carry a choice can be compared in the same way.
